[{:registry/operating-states [:intake :propose :approve :execute :audit], :registry/required-technologies [:robotics :identity :audit-ledger], :actor/scope-excluded-ops [:finalize-fumigation-decision :finalize-pesticide-application-decision :finalize-pesticide-application :authorize-pesticide-application :authorize-fumigation :proceed-with-pesticide-application :proceed-with-fumigation :finalize-chemical-safety-clearance :declare-site-safe-for-reentry :declare-treated-site-safe-for-reentry :clear-site-for-reentry :override-site-safety-officer-judgment :override-safety-officer-judgment], :actor/robotics? true, :adr/related ["2799007535-cloud-itonami-isco-7535-pelt-dressers-tanners-fellmongers-coverage" "2799007521-cloud-itonami-isco-7521-wood-treaters-coverage"], :adr/status :accepted, :actor/scope-excluded-terms ["proceed with the pesticide application" "proceed with the fumigation" "authorize the pesticide application" "authorize the fumigation" "finalize the fumigation decision" "finalize the pesticide application decision" "finalize the pesticide application" "declare the site safe for re-entry" "declare the treated site safe for re-entry" "clear the site for re-entry" "clear the treated site for re-entry" "finalize the chemical safety clearance" "finalize the chemical-safety clearance" "declare the site chemical-safety cleared" "declare the treated site chemical safety cleared" "override the site safety officer's judgment" "override the safety officer's judgment" "override site safety officer judgment"], :adr/id 2799007544, :phase/rollout "{0 {:label \"read-only\", :writes #{}, :auto #{}}, 1 {:label \"assisted-logging\", :writes #{:log-work-record}, :auto #{}}, 2 {:label \"assisted-coordination\", :writes #{:schedule-crew-operation :coordinate-supply-order :log-work-record}, :auto #{}}, 3 {:label \"supervised-auto\", :writes #{:flag-safety-concern :schedule-crew-operation :coordinate-supply-order :log-work-record}, :auto #{:schedule-crew-operation :coordinate-supply-order :log-work-record}}}", :actor/domain :services/pest-weed-control-scheduling-logistics-coordination, :actor/proposal-ops [:log-work-record :schedule-crew-operation :flag-safety-concern :coordinate-supply-order], :actor/hard-checks [:applicator-verified :site-verified :effect-propose :op-not-allowed :scope-exclusion], :registry/maturity :implemented, :actor/isco-08 "7544", :actor/always-escalate-ops [:flag-safety-concern], :adr/doc_type "adr", :operation/nodes [:intake :advise :govern :decide :request-approval :commit :hold], :operation/entry-point :intake, :adr/body "# ADR 2799007544: cloud-itonami ISCO-08 7544 Fumigators and Other Pest and Weed Controllers pest-control/fumigation-operation scheduling/logistics coordination coverage\n\n## Status\n\nAccepted\n\n## Context\n\nThis is part of the occupation-classification track (sibling to the\n`cloud-itonami-isic-*` industry-actor rollout, same governing\nADR-2607121000): codify every ISCO-08 occupation unit group as an\nautonomous \"actor\" (LLM/advisor behind an independent Governor, a\n`langgraph.graph` StateGraph, append-only audit ledger), modeled on a\nrobotics premise -- a robot performs the occupation's physical/\nadministrative work, proposing actions to an independent Governor that\ngates them, never dispatches hardware itself, always escalates\nhigh-stakes decisions to a human. This ADR covers **ISCO-08 class 7544\n-- Fumigators and Other Pest and Weed Controllers** only, Wave3\n(production/trades) of the rollout (Wave0/1/2 100% complete, Wave3\nbatches 1-10 already landed: 60 occupations so far).\n\nThe `kotoba-lang/occupation` registry's live `{:id \"7544\" ...}` entry\nwas verified against a fresh clone before any work began (`clojure -M\n-e \"(require 'kotoba.occupation) (println (kotoba.occupation/get-occupation\n\\\"7544\\\"))\"` from a checkout with the `kotoba-lang/technology`\nsibling present at the correct `../technology` relative path), and its\n`:name` read exactly \"Fumigators and Other Pest and Weed Controllers\"\n-- confirming the premise, no mismatch. Prior to this ADR the entry's\n`:maturity` was `:spec` with `:required-technologies [:robotics\n:identity :audit-ledger]` and no `:repo`/`:business-id`; no repository\nexisted at `cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-7544` (`gh api` 404\nconfirmed before scaffolding). This ADR and its companion registry\nedit promote the `7544` entry to `:implemented`.\n\nFumigators and other pest and weed controllers apply pesticides and\nfumigants (toxic-chemical agents) to control pests, weeds and other\norganisms -- real toxic-chemical-exposure hazard to both the\napplicator and building occupants/environment if application\nprotocols are not followed. The controlling constraint for this\nvertical is that the closed op allowlist must NEVER include any op\nthat directly finalizes a fumigation/pesticide-application-execution\ndecision or a chemical-safety-clearance decision (declaring a treated\nsite safe for re-entry), nor override a site safety officer's\njudgment -- those decisions must always be a hard permanent block,\nnever auto-commit-eligible. This actor is deliberately scoped to\nPEST-CONTROL/FUMIGATION-OPERATION SCHEDULING/LOGISTICS COORDINATION\nONLY -- it never applies pesticides or fumigants itself, and it never\nmakes a chemical-safety-clearance/re-entry decision itself.\n\nThe reference implementation mirrored for module SHAPE and the\nclosed-allowlist + independently-verified-provenance safety pattern is\n[`cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-7535`](https://github.com/cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-7535)\n(Pelt Dressers, Tanners and Fellmongers: `store`/`advisor`/`governor`/\n`actor` split, `langgraph.graph` StateGraph, full doc set, same wave,\na closely-related chemical-processing hazard pattern --\nexecution-finalization and chemical-safety-clearance exclusion, the\nsame structural shape this vertical needs for fumigation/pesticide-\napplication-execution and chemical-safety-clearance/re-entry\nexclusion) -- cloned and read in full in this session.\n\n## Decision\n\nPublish `cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-7544` as an OSS pest-control/\nfumigation-operation scheduling/logistics-coordination actor for\nfumigators and other pest and weed controllers, following the SAME\ngoverned-actor architecture as every prior `cloud-itonami-isco-*`\nactor in this fleet: **Pest and Weed Control Scheduling Coordination\nAdvisor ⊣ PestCoordGovernor** on a `langgraph.graph` StateGraph\n(`io.github.kotoba-lang/langgraph`, git-sha-pinned identically to the\nreference's `deps.edn` -- a different library coordinate from the\n`langgraph-clj` used by the unrelated `20-actors/*` actor family),\nwith an independent Governor, human-in-the-loop approval via\n`interrupt-before`, and an append-only audit ledger.\n\nThis actor is deliberately **pest-control/fumigation-operation\nscheduling/logistics coordination ONLY, never direct fumigation/\npesticide-application-execution or chemical-safety-clearance\nauthority** -- it must NEVER be designed to exercise, simulate\nexercising, or propose exercising a fumigation/pesticide-application-\nexecution decision, a chemical-safety-clearance decision, or any\noverride of a site safety officer's judgment. These are not merely\ngated behind escalation -- they are structurally absent from the\nop-allowlist entirely.\n\n### Closed proposal-op allowlist (all `:effect :propose`)\n\n- `:log-work-record` -- job/site/progress data logging (administrative\n  metadata only)\n- `:schedule-crew-operation` -- crew/site/task scheduling proposal\n- `:flag-safety-concern` -- surface an exposure-risk/site-condition/\n  equipment-condition concern, ALWAYS escalates, NEVER auto-commit\n  eligible\n- `:coordinate-supply-order` -- administrative/equipment procurement\n  proposal (NOT pesticide/fumigant chemicals themselves -- chemical\n  procurement/handling is entirely out of scope for this\n  administrative-coordination actor)\n\nThis actor has **NO op, anywhere in its allowlist, that resembles** a\nfumigation/pesticide-application-execution decision, a chemical-\nsafety-clearance decision, or a site-safety-officer-judgment override.\nThese are **structurally absent from the op-allowlist entirely**, not\nmerely gated behind escalation -- an op such as\n`:finalize-fumigation-decision` or `:declare-site-safe-for-reentry`\ncannot even be proposed by the reference mock advisor (its\n`infer`/`rationale-for` functions only ever emit one of the four\nallowlist ops), and if any code path (a forged proposal, a future LLM\nadvisor, a misbehaving fork) ever produced such an op, the governor's\nclosed-allowlist HARD check (`:unknown-op`) rejects it unconditionally\nand permanently. A second, independent HARD check\n(`:scope-excluded-action`) names eleven concretely-forbidden ops\n(`pestcoord.governor/scope-excluded-ops`:\n`:finalize-fumigation-decision`,\n`:finalize-pesticide-application-decision`,\n`:finalize-pesticide-application`, `:authorize-pesticide-application`,\n`:authorize-fumigation`, `:proceed-with-pesticide-application`,\n`:proceed-with-fumigation`, `:finalize-chemical-safety-clearance`,\n`:declare-site-safe-for-reentry`,\n`:declare-treated-site-safe-for-reentry`, `:clear-site-for-reentry`,\n`:override-site-safety-officer-judgment`,\n`:override-safety-officer-judgment`) as defense-in-depth, and is\nverified by dedicated unit tests\n(`pestcoord.governor-test/hard-on-scope-excluded-op-finalize-fumigation-decision`\nand its eleven siblings), plus actor-level integration coverage\n(`pestcoord.actor-test/holds-a-scope-excluded-op-even-at-high-confidence`,\n`.../holds-a-chemical-safety-clearance-op-even-at-high-confidence`,\n`.../holds-an-override-site-safety-officer-op-even-at-high-confidence`).\n\n### Three HARD governor checks (permanent, un-overridable, plus the closed-allowlist check)\n\n1. **Applicator unregistered** -- any proposal must resolve to an\n   independently registered applicator record (`:no-applicator`).\n   Never trusts the proposal's own claim of who the applicator is.\n2. **Site unregistered** -- any proposal must cite an independently\n   registered treatment site (`:no-site`). Together with check 1, this\n   directly implements the CRITICAL invariant that a worker/site\n   record must be independently verified/registered before any\n   action.\n3. **Effect not `:propose`** -- any other `:effect` value is a HARD\n   block (a claim to directly actuate outside governance)\n   (`:no-actuation`).\n4. **Op not allowed / scope-excluded action** -- any `:op` outside the\n   closed four-op allowlist is a HARD, PERMANENT block (`:unknown-op`),\n   and independently, any proposal whose `:op` names one of the\n   concretely-forbidden ops above, OR whose free-text `:rationale`\n   names a finalization/execution action for proceeding with a\n   fumigation/pesticide application, declaring a site safe for\n   re-entry, or overriding a site safety officer's judgment, is a\n   second HARD, PERMANENT block (`:scope-excluded-action`), evaluated\n   unconditionally on content -- defense-in-depth on top of the\n   allowlist check: even if a future change somehow widened the\n   allowlist, content-based rejection still independently blocks\n   fumigation/pesticide-application-execution/chemical-safety-\n   clearance/safety-override language wherever it appears in free\n   text.\n\n### One always-escalate (SOFT) gate, one threshold gate, plus the general confidence floor\n\n- `:flag-safety-concern` -- ALWAYS escalates to a human, regardless of\n  confidence (governor's implicit always-risky check). This is the\n  ONLY channel by which the robot may surface an exposure-risk/site-\n  condition/equipment-condition observation -- it never resolves the\n  concern itself, and the robot's role ends at \"here is the hazard\n  data,\" never \"here is what I recommend doing about site safety or\n  chemical-safety clearance.\"\n- a `:coordinate-supply-order` proposal whose `:cost` exceeds\n  `supply-cost-threshold` (2000, inclusive boundary verified by\n  `ok-supply-order-at-threshold-boundary`) -- always needs human\n  sign-off, independent of confidence. This is an ESCALATION, not a\n  hard block, per the task's explicit domain design: an\n  over-threshold administrative/equipment supply order is not itself\n  unsafe, unlike a fumigation/pesticide-application-execution or\n  chemical-safety-clearance attempt, which is why it is handled by a\n  different mechanism (soft escalate vs. structural absence).\n- (advisor confidence below `confidence-floor` 0.6 also escalates, for\n  any op.)\n\n### Self-trip guard (fleet-known bug class, carried over from the sibling ISIC/ISCO tracks)\n\nA governor's scope-exclusion term list phrased as a bare noun (e.g.\n\"pesticide\", \"fumigant\", \"chemical\") can accidentally match inside the\nmock advisor's own default rationale text for a legitimate proposal,\nself-blocking the actor's own happy path -- this is exactly the risk\nthis vertical's own vocabulary (a pest-control coordination actor\nworking adjacent to chemical application work necessarily discusses\nthose concepts to do its scheduling/logging job) could trigger if\nphrased carelessly. `pestcoord.governor/scope-excluded-phrases` is\nphrased exclusively as finalization/execution ACTIONS (e.g. \"proceed\nwith the pesticide application\", \"finalize the fumigation decision\",\n\"declare the site safe for re-entry\", \"override the site safety\nofficer's judgment\" -- matching the task's own worked example\nphrasing), never bare nouns, and\n`pestcoord.governor-test/default-mock-advisor-proposals-never-self-trip-on-scope-exclusion`\nis a dedicated regression test asserting every op in the closed\nallowlist, driven through the default mock advisor, never trips\n`:scope-excluded-action`. The mock advisor's own default rationale\ntemplate was deliberately written to contain the bare nouns (\"logged\nwork record for applicator ...\", \"scheduled crew operation for pest\ncontrol task ...\", \"... routed for site safety officer review\")\nWITHOUT containing any of the full finalization-action phrases, so the\nregression test is a genuine stress test of the bare-noun-vs-action-\nphrase distinction, not a vacuous pass. Green on first run in this\nsession -- the scope-exclusion self-trip guard itself was never\ntripped during development.\n`pestcoord.actor-test/holds-a-scope-excluded-op-even-at-high-confidence`\nand its chemical-safety-clearance/site-safety-officer-override\nsiblings additionally exercise the same invariant through the full\ngraph run, confirming a scope-excluded proposal never reaches\n`commit-record!` regardless of disposition path.\n\n## Consequences\n\n- `kotoba-lang/occupation` registry's `{:id \"7544\" ...}` entry promoted\n  from `:spec` to `:implemented`, with `:repo`/`:business-id` set to\n  the real repository\n  (`https://github.com/cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-7544` /\n  `cloud-itonami-isco-7544`), and `maturity-summary`'s implemented\n  count bumped accordingly (recomputed live from the registry via\n  `kotoba.occupation/maturity-summary`, not assumed, immediately\n  before the edit).\n- Full test suite (`clojure -M:test`): 34 tests, 73 assertions, 0\n  failures, 0 errors -- raw output captured in this ADR's\n  `:test/counts` and in the task report, independently re-verified\n  against a brand-new fresh clone of the pushed repository (not just\n  the local working copy).\n- No `manifest/west.yml` changes -- this actor is registered purely via\n  the `kotoba-lang/occupation` capability registry, consistent with\n  sibling `cloud-itonami-isco-*`/`cloud-itonami-isic-*` actors.\n- No `kotoba-lang/technology` dependency was added to the actor's\n  `deps.edn` -- only the central `kotoba-lang/occupation` registry\n  itself depends on it, to resolve `:required-technologies` stacks.\n- This ADR is EDN-only, matching the live `90-docs/adr` convention\n  (commit `6d2d0eeda658`, \"Remove all .md, normalize ADRs to\n  tx-data\") -- a single-entity EDN vector with an `:adr/body` string\n  blob rather than a paired `.md`+`.edn`.\n\n## Alternatives considered\n\n- Giving the actor an op like `:approve-site-clearance` that is merely\n  gated behind an always-escalate list: rejected outright per the\n  task's explicit domain-design guidance -- an always-escalate op\n  still means the *capability* to finalize a chemical-safety-clearance\n  decision exists in the code path and is merely deferred to a human\n  gate. For a decision this severe (toxic-chemical-exposure risk from\n  pesticide/fumigant application, affecting both applicator and\n  building occupants/environment), the capability itself must not\n  exist. The only op related to site-safety escalation is\n  `:flag-safety-concern`, which surfaces the need for human/site-\n  safety-officer attention and never itself decides, authorizes, or\n  defers a fumigation/pesticide-application-execution or chemical-\n  safety-clearance decision for later automatic execution.\n- Making the supply-cost ceiling a HARD block (mirroring the\n  transaction-amount-ceiling HARD check used by some sibling actors):\n  rejected per the task's explicit domain design -- an over-threshold\n  administrative/equipment supply order is not itself unsafe, so it is\n  handled as a soft escalate gate, not a hard block; only the\n  fumigation/pesticide-application-execution/chemical-safety-\n  clearance/site-safety-officer-override class is a hard, unconditional\n  block.\n- A bare-noun scope-exclusion term list (e.g. `[\"pesticide\" \"fumigant\"\n  \"chemical\"]`): rejected outright per the fleet-known self-trip bug\n  class described above (and CLAUDE.md's own worked example for this\n  exact task), and per the explicit instruction to phrase\n  scope-exclusion terms as finalization/execution ACTIONS, never bare\n  nouns -- this actor's own domain necessarily discusses pesticide/\n  fumigant/chemical/site-safety-officer concepts to do its\n  scheduling/logging job, so a bare-noun list would risk self-blocking\n  the actor's own legitimate proposals (regression-tested directly\n  against rationale text that deliberately contains those bare nouns).\n- Naming only a single fumigation-execution-finalization op family\n  (mirroring a narrower single-category exclusion shape used by some\n  sibling actors): rejected -- the task's domain design names two\n  distinct excluded decision categories (fumigation/pesticide-\n  application-execution, chemical-safety-clearance/re-entry) plus a\n  site-safety-officer-judgment override, so this ADR and the\n  governor's `scope-excluded-ops`/`scope-excluded-phrases` name both\n  explicitly (with fumigation and pesticide-application variants for\n  the execution category, and re-entry-clearance phrasing for the\n  chemical-safety-clearance category) rather than conflating them,\n  giving each its own dedicated unit test.\n- Merging `applicator` and `site` into a single entity (only checking\n  one provenance record): rejected -- the two-entity provenance shape\n  (independently-registered applicator AND independently-registered\n  site) matches the CRITICAL invariant that a \"worker/facility record\n  must be independently verified/registered before any action\" and\n  the established pattern from prior `cloud-itonami-isco-*` actors\n  with two-sided provenance checks.\n\n## Itemized safety confirmation\n\nEvery op in this actor's closed proposal-op allowlist, and why it is\nsafe under the constraints above:\n\n1. **`:log-work-record`** -- job/site/progress data logging only\n   (administrative metadata). Requires an independently registered\n   applicator AND site (`:no-applicator`/`:no-site`). Not a\n   fumigation/pesticide-application-execution or chemical-safety-\n   clearance decision in any respect -- it is a records-management\n   action.\n2. **`:schedule-crew-operation`** -- crew/site/task scheduling\n   proposal only. Cannot carry a fumigation/pesticide-application-\n   execution or chemical-safety-clearance decision (structurally\n   absent from the op's field shape; any such attempt via free text is\n   caught by the `:scope-excluded-action` content check). Does not\n   authorize, finalize, or draw any conclusion about a specific\n   application or re-entry clearance -- it is a calendar/logistics\n   action.\n3. **`:flag-safety-concern`** -- surfaces an exposure-risk/site-\n   condition/equipment-condition concern to a human/site safety\n   officer; ALWAYS escalates, unconditionally, at every confidence\n   level, NEVER auto-commit eligible. This is the ONLY channel through\n   which a safety observation may be surfaced, and it explicitly never\n   resolves, decides, or acts on anything itself -- the site safety\n   officer reviews and acts entirely on their own authority, including\n   any chemical-safety-clearance/re-entry decision.\n4. **`:coordinate-supply-order`** -- administrative/equipment\n   procurement proposal only (item, cost) -- NOT pesticide/fumigant\n   chemicals themselves, which are entirely out of scope for this\n   administrative-coordination actor. Not a fumigation/pesticide-\n   application-execution, chemical-safety-clearance or authorization\n   decision of any kind -- it is a purchasing-logistics action, gated\n   by ordinary cost-ceiling escalation (above 2000 always escalates).\n\nNo op in this allowlist, and no op anywhere in this codebase, resembles\na fumigation/pesticide-application-execution decision (e.g. deciding\nto proceed with a specific fumigation or pesticide application), a\nchemical-safety-clearance decision (e.g. declaring a treated site safe\nfor re-entry), or a site-safety-officer-judgment override -- these\ncategories are structurally absent, not gated, confirmed by: (a) the\nclosed four-op allowlist itself (`pestcoord.governor/allowed-ops`),\n(b) thirteen concretely-named forbidden-op fixtures rejected at the\ngovernor-unit level (`pestcoord.governor/scope-excluded-ops`,\n`pestcoord.governor-test/hard-on-scope-excluded-op-finalize-fumigation-decision`\nand its siblings), and (c) a content-based scope-exclusion check\nindependent of the `:op` field\n(`pestcoord.governor/scope-excluded-phrases`,\n`.../contains-excluded-phrase?`), tested directly against a rationale\nthat attempts to proceed with a fumigation/pesticide application,\nfinalize the pesticide application, declare a site safe for re-entry,\ndeclare the site chemical-safety cleared, or override a site safety\nofficer's judgment even via an otherwise-allowlisted op\n(`pestcoord.governor-test/hard-on-scope-excluded-rationale-proceed-with-fumigation`,\n`.../hard-on-scope-excluded-rationale-declare-site-safe-for-reentry`,\n`.../hard-on-scope-excluded-rationale-finalize-pesticide-application`,\n`.../hard-on-scope-excluded-rationale-override-site-safety-officer`,\n`.../hard-on-scope-excluded-rationale-declare-site-chemical-safety-cleared`).\n", :adr/date "2026-07-18", :actor/cost-threshold-escalate-ops "{:coordinate-supply-order 2000}", :test/counts "{:total 34, :assertions 73, :failures 0, :errors 0}", :actor/coordination? true, :actor/name "Pest and Weed Control Scheduling and Logistics Coordination Practice", :db/id -1, :test/coverage [:governor-hard-checks :advisor-shapes :integration :store-contract :scope-exclusion-self-trip-regression :applicator-verification :site-verification :closed-op-allowlist :supply-cost-threshold-escalation], :registry/business-id "cloud-itonami-isco-7544", :registry/repo "https://github.com/cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-7544", :adr/scope [:kotoba-lang/occupation :cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-7544], :test/simulator :pestcoord.actor-test, :adr/title "cloud-itonami-isco-7544 — Fumigators and Other Pest and Weed Controllers Pest-Control/Fumigation-Operation Scheduling/Logistics Coordination Coverage", :adr/source-format "edn", :adr/references [2607121000], :actor/id :cloud-itonami-isco-7544}]